post("PL-02", "planning", "reel",
     "“Alterations to the front elevation comprising replacement fenestration”",
     "“ALTERATIONS TO THE FRONT ELEVATION COMPRISING REPLACEMENT FENESTRATION.”\n\nRead that on a planning portal at 10pm and tell me you do not need a translation.\n\n“Hi. We’d like to replace some windows. Front of house. There are windows.”\n\nThat application is four years old. It has a case officer, a consultee, a decision notice and a set of approved drawings. And it is a straightforward, well-specified, confident job for somebody with the right eyes on it.",
     "Photorealistic 9:16 shot of a laptop on a dark desk displaying a dense planning document with a highlighted line, a cold coffee, a notebook, the glow of the screen, late-night research mood, moody and serious",
     "screen-mock", "planning", img="9:16", cta="Search, filter, and turn the application into a lead. fenora.pro")

post("PL-03", "planning", "static",
     "“Accepted in principle.”",
     "“The application is acceptable in principle subject to…”\n\nAnd then there is a list of six things, and one of them is that you must demonstrate by written justification that slim-profile uPVC is acceptable in place of the original crittal windows, which takes eleven paragraphs and a photo of a window on a street four miles away.\n\nYou are now selling a window and writing a heritage statement. There is no training for this. This is just Tuesday.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a Victorian UK terraced house in a conservation area, a planning officer's report pinned to a board beside it, an old Crittall window on the neighbouring property, overcast light, dry and observational",
     "photo-real", "planning")

post("PL-04", "planning", "carousel",
     "What Article 4 actually does (in a way nobody explains it)",
     "Slide 1: Normally, in England, replacing windows in a house is permitted development. No permission. Just do it.\n\nSlide 2: Article 4 takes that right away from you in a specific direction — usually the front — usually to protect a view.\n\nSlide 3: In a conservation area the same applies, and in Scotland since May 2024, prior notification applies to a lot of listed and street-facing windows.\n\nSlide 4: On a listed building you need listed building consent and uPVC is basically never acceptable.\n\nSlide 5: The mistake: finding out all of this after you have surveyed, specified and quoted.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a UK conservation area streetscape with Georgian terraces, a red-bordered conservation area sign visible, overcast light, documentary, understated",
     "photo-real", "planning", cta="Conservation area, Article 4 and listed status, flagged before you knock. fenora.pro")

