post("PL-17", "planning", "static",
     "The 8-mile radius. Try 8 first.",
     "Start at 8 miles.\n\nNot 25. Not the county. Eight miles, twelve months, window and door work only.\n\nEight miles is an hour of driving, so it is a radius you can genuinely serve. Eight miles is small enough that you know every skip hire firm in it. Eight miles is large enough that there is work every week.\n\nThen when you are consistently converting, go to 15. Then 25. Then open the second branch.\n\nThe radius is the whole strategy. Everything else is detail.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a UK road with a single person standing in the middle holding a phone, a map of a small circle around them, early morning light, sense of quiet decision-making, wide and cinematic",
     "photo-real", "planning", cta="Start with 8 miles. fenora.pro/job-finder")

post("PL-18", "planning", "static",
     "The 5:42pm panic email.",
     "At 5:42pm on the Friday, a planning approval lands.\n\nAn extension. Three weeks of site work. Nine casements and a bifold to a client who went quiet three weeks ago.\n\nThe office closes at six. The job starts Monday. Nobody has a price for this. The glass is a nine-day lead time. Somebody’s phone is about to ring and they are going to say “how much?”\n\nThe whole business now depends on whether anyone can turn a planning email into a priced quote in eleven minutes.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of an office at dusk, one screen lit with a planning approval email, a coffee cup, a clock showing just before 6pm, a blurred figure standing up urgently, warm desk lamp against blue window light, cinematic tension",
     "screen-mock", "planning", cta="Planning approval to priced quote, in minutes. fenora.pro")

post("PL-19", "planning", "static",
     "“The application is for the house next door.”",
     "Every single person searching a planning portal finds this eventually.\n\nThe address is right. The description is right. The applicant is right. It is the wrong house, the wrong plot, or a house that does not exist yet, or a plot that was sold three times.\n\nThe map pins to the street rather than the property. The applicant field is a land registry placeholder. The plans are a site in Essex and the work is in Devon.\n\nCheck the plan, not the address. It takes ten seconds and it is the difference between a quote and an apology.",
     "Photorealistic photograph of a printed site location plan spread on a desk with a red circle on the wrong plot, a site visit clipboard, a hi-vis jacket in the corner, forensic and dry, cool office light",
     "photo-real", "planning")

